+# The Belief Lab
+
+Mid-career beliefs are presented as thoughts that can be examined rather than facts that must be obeyed. The lesson moves from three constraining statements through a clear reframe—leverage, experiments, and re-design—before ending with one practical question.
+
+## Visual approach
+
+- A consistent navy workspace keeps the lesson calm, credible, and focused.
+- Beliefs appear as labeled cards that are opened, challenged, and replaced.
+- Gold marks the active choice; blue carries supporting structure and progress.
+- Each beat uses one dominant visual metaphor: belief cards, a leverage fulcrum, experiment paths, adjustable role blocks, a belief-to-action bridge, and a final experiment card.
+
+## Motion approach
+
+One paused, seekable timeline drives all motion. Cards enter with short vertical reveals, rules draw left-to-right, and diagrams build in the order the narration introduces them. Transitions are direct and restrained, with no looping motion.
